<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhadradri Kothagudem district</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhadradri Kothagudem district is a district in the north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, created in 2016 from part of the former Khammam district. Its headquarters is Kothagudem, and it includes the temple town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhadrachalam">Bhadrachalam</a> and the industrial town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palvancha">Palvancha</a>. Crossed by the Godavari, it is heavily forested and rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, mined by the Singareni Collieries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhadradri-kothagudem-district.jpg" alt="Bhadradri Kothagudem district" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adityamadhav83 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_View_of_Bhadrachalam_Gopuram.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bhadradri Kothagudem is the largest district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, with an area of 7,483 square kilometres. Within the state it borders the districts of Khammam to the south, Mahabubabad to the west and Mulugu to the northwest. To the north and northeast lie the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> and Sukma districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a>, and to the east and southeast the Polavaram and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eluru">Eluru</a> districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>. The district is rich in forest reserves and contains the Kinnerasani Wildlife Sanctuary, and its ground holds important minerals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> above all.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Prehistoric people probably ranged over the lower Godavari valley around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhadrachalam">Bhadrachalam</a>, Kothagudem, Pinapaka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palvancha">Paloncha</a>. Megalithic dolmens have been found at Janampet in Pinapaka, and the villages of Padugonigudem in Gundala have yielded megalithic remains discovered in recent exploration. Buddhist sites are known at Aswaraopeta and at Karukonda near Kothagudem.</p><p>The town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhadrachalam">Bhadrachalam</a> has a documented history centred on its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> temple, built around the 17th century by Kancherla Gopanna, a saint-poet better known as Bhakta Ramadasu or Bhadrachala Ramadasu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The district has two revenue divisions, Kothagudem and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhadrachalam">Bhadrachalam</a>, 481 gram <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panchayat">panchayats</a> and five municipalities, and five constituencies of the Telangana Legislative Assembly. Aswaraopeta, Bhadrachalam, Yellandu and Pinapaka are reserved for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-tribes">Scheduled Tribes</a>; Kothagudem and Aswaraopeta belong to the Khammam Lok Sabha constituency and the other three to Mahabubabad.</p><p>Mining and industry shape its economy. The Singareni Collieries Company, a government <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> company owned jointly by the Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a>, has its headquarters in Kothagudem. The Kothagudem Thermal Power Station at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palvancha">Palvancha</a> is one of the coal-fired plants of the Telangana State Power Generation Corporation, and the Paperboards and Specialty Papers Division of ITC Limited works at Sarapaka near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhadrachalam">Bhadrachalam</a>.</p><p>At the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> the district had 1,069,261 inhabitants, about a third of them in towns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-tribes">Scheduled Tribes</a> made up 36.66% of the population and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-castes">Scheduled Castes</a> 13.42%. Telugu was the first language of 72.34% of the people, followed by Koya, Lambadi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and Hindi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhadradri Kothagudem district” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
